{
 "name": "poly_split_parity",
 "description": "Splitting a face across two of its vertices, frozen from the reference on open geometry only. Every case here CUTS: the refusals this command shares with the reference are a separate fixture, because a refusal frozen as parity would pass with the command removed.",
 "source": "frozen reference capture (task 1121, nine-tool sweep)",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
  "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
  "method": "command",
  "captured_utc": "2026-08-18",
  "harness": "tools/local/capture (tasks 1121/1123)",
  "task": "1160",
  "notes": "Chord chosen to separate candidate laws, not to be easy: inside and outside a non-convex pentagon, a quad whose neighbours share both endpoints, an n-gon produced by an earlier merge. Geometry AND the post-operation selection agreed in the capture, so both are frozen. CHANNELS: the sweep's comparison was never vertex-only -- it compared the vertex multiset, the edge multiset, faces BOTH as ordered rings (winding shows) and as unordered position sets, the material partition, the per-face 'did this face's own tag change', and the post-command selection. Re-checked independently over all 215 frozen cases: ZERO agree on vertices while differing on ordered face rings, so nothing frozen here is a vertex-channel-only agreement. PRECISION: the sweep compared at 1e-4 and this fixture's tolerance matches it. The actual reproduction is far tighter -- 208 of the 215 cases are bit-identical at the dumps' 6 decimals and the remaining 7 agree to 3e-6 or better, that being the worst deviation anywhere in the set. The tolerance is the MEASUREMENT's bound, not the data's."
 },
